+## Waiting after 429 (the Retry-After header)
+
+A 429 answer means "too many requests"; the server may add a `Retry-After` header that says how long to wait.
+
+* Retry-After decision. A `Retry-After` header is read as seconds by one rule: digits with an optional decimal part (a decimal part is accepted), spaces and tabs around it removed, ASCII only. Anything longer than 3600 seconds is cut to 3600 seconds (`RETRY_AFTER_MAX_SECONDS` in `constants.py`), also a number too big for a float. A Retry-After that is an HTTP date is read as no value: the tools then use their own wait. The three copies of the rule (`envnum.retry_after_seconds`, `load_driver._retry_after_fallback`, `hubspot_probe._retry_after`) are tested to agree.
